          ],
          "text": "The central legal question is whether, prior to the 1929 enactment of Section 65-A of the Transfer of Property Act, a mortgagor in possession had the power to grant a permanent lease of the mortgaged property so as to bind the mortgagee.",
          "refs": [
            "S34eef73a1804"
          ],
          "source_owner": "O64a6f8caf1b4a2e1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "Tc0465f0d4cbe91b2": {
      "uses": [
        {
          "state": "TRUE",
          "contribution": "The High Court held the lease valid because it did not render the Bank's security insufficient under Section 66, though this assessment was not challenged by the Privy Council.",
          "limitations": [
            "SR34d2fe4ad826c718|LEGAL_ASSESSMENT:COURT_RECORDED:PARTIAL_SUPPORT",
            "SR34d2fe4ad826c718|LEGAL_ASSESSMENT:COURT_RECORDED:PARTIAL_SUPPORT"
          ],
          "content_role": "LEGAL_ASSESSMENT",
          "status": "COURT_RECORDED",
          "purpose": "PARTIAL_SUPPORT",
          "source_record_ids": [
            "SR34d2fe4ad826c718"
          ],
          "text": "The High Court negatived the contention regarding constructive res judicata. It considered whether Wazir Narayan Singh had power to grant the permanent lease, concluding the lease did not render the Bank's security insufficient under Section 66.",
          "refs": [
            "S2d91d1862dc2",
            "Sd3c548138ec6",
            "S978119f4c532"
          ],
          "source_owner": "Oc4f8cbb5279d28a3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "Tb6cbe9b73d77f4df": {
      "uses": [
        {
          "state": "TRUE",
          "contribution": "The trial court found the plaintiff subrogated to the Bank's position and the defendants bound by the mortgage decree, but the High Court negatived constructive res judicata.",
          "limitations": [
            "SR89feb810c247d711|LEGAL_ASSESSMENT:COURT_RECORDED:BACKGROUND",
            "SR89feb810c247d711|LEGAL_ASSESSMENT:COURT_RECORDED:BACKGROUND"
          ],
          "content_role": "LEGAL_ASSESSMENT",
          "status": "COURT_RECORDED",
          "purpose": "BACKGROUND",
          "source_record_ids": [
            "SR89feb810c247d711"
          ],
          "text": "The Trial Court held the Plaintiff was subrogated to the Bank's position, Defendant 1 was the Manager/Karta, Defendant 2 was fully represented, the decree was binding, and the Plaintiff was entitled to recover possession and mesne profits.",
          "refs": [
            "S601d5df39aca"
          ],
          "source_owner": "Oc4f8cbb5279d28a3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "T972ace70bb20383b": {
      "uses": [
        {
          "state": "TRUE",
          "contribution": "The defendants argued the 1925 permanent lease was not binding because they were not parties to the mortgage suit and the auction sale could not end the lease.",
          "limitations": [
            "SR43b1f9b4f4974e70|ATTRIBUTION:PARTY_CLAIM:REPORT_ASSERTION",
            "SR43b1f9b4f4974e70|ATTRIBUTION:PARTY_CLAIM:REPORT_ASSERTION"
          ],
          "content_role": "ATTRIBUTION",
          "status": "PARTY_CLAIM",
          "purpose": "REPORT_ASSERTION",
          "source_record_ids": [
            "SR43b1f9b4f4974e70"
          ],
          "text": "Defendant 2 contended that the Plaintiff was not subrogated to the Bank's position, the decree was not binding as he was not a party, the Patta was not ended by the auction sale, and a partition occurred within a year allotting properties to him.",
          "refs": [
            "S2e0ba5982107",
            "Sf05a6bb45a8e",
            "Sd6e9439ddc2d"
          ],
          "source_owner": "Oc4f8cbb5279d28a3"
